<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Masts</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">654 or 655</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-byzantine-wars">Arab–Byzantine wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Masts was a naval battle fought in 654 or 655 off the coast of Lycia in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, in which the young Arab fleet built up under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muawiya-i">Muawiya</a> as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, led by Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> ibn Sa'd, defeated a Byzantine fleet commanded by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a> in person. The first great naval victory of the Muslims, it ended unchallenged Byzantine dominance of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-masts.jpg" alt="Battle of the Masts" width="320" height="472" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tarikhuna_bi-uslub_qasasi-Battle_of_Dh%C4%81t_al-%E1%B9%A2aw%C4%81r%C4%AB.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the 650s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> completed its conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> and went on expanding into Byzantine territory. In 645 Caliph Uthman appointed Abd Allah ibn Sa'd governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, replacing the semi-independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a>. Uthman let Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, raid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> in 649, and the success of that campaign made naval activity from Egypt possible. Abd Allah built a strong navy and proved a skilled commander, and under him the Muslim navy won a number of victories, including repulsing a Byzantine counterattack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 646.</p><p>The campaign that led to the battle was the earliest attempt by Mu'awiya to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. He led an expedition into Cappadocia while his fleet advanced along the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constans-ii">Constans II</a> embarked against it with a large fleet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The fleets met off Mount Phoenix in Lycia, near the harbour of Phoenix, modern Finike. Theophanes the Confessor, a Byzantine chronicler of the 9th century, tells that Constans dreamed the night before that he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>. An interpreter read the name as thes allo nike, give victory to another. In rough seas, al-Tabari says, the ships were arranged in lines and lashed together so that the men could fight hand to hand. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> won, though losses were heavy on both sides, and Constans barely escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, according to Theophanes by exchanging uniforms with one of his officers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Traditional Arabic sources record no further attacks, and the respite granted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> is usually put down to the Arab fleet retreating amid conflict over Uthman's authority, the first stirrings of civil war. The Armenian historian Sebeos reports instead that the fleet went on to attempt a siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, which a fierce storm defeated by sinking the ships carrying siege machines. On this account Mu'awiya's land force, which had reached Chalcedon, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> without its artillery.</p><p>Both fleets suffered major casualties. The battle is described as the first decisive conflict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> on the deep sea. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, long a Roman lake, became contested between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> and the Eastern Roman Empire, and Muslim naval power later reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and cleared the way for uncontested expansion along the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Masts” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Muawiyah himself had taken a leading part in these conquests as governor of Syria, where he built the first Arab war fleet, raided Cyprus and Rhodes, and defeated the Byzantine navy at the <b>Battle of the Masts</b> in 654 or 655.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
